WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.777
Secure serialization in .

2
00:00:01.777 --> 00:00:04.691
NET is a repeatable engineering workflow.

3
00:00:04.691 --> 00:00:10.661
Start by identifying where objects become data and where data becomes objects again.

4
00:00:10.661 --> 00:00:20.256
Look at APIs, queues, files, caches, cookies, sessions, imports, exports, logs, telemetry, integrations, and stored serialized records.

5
00:00:20.256 --> 00:00:24.094
Those paths are boundary decisions, not just plumbing.

6
00:00:24.094 --> 00:00:29.282
Prefer explicit contracts and maintained framework-supported serializers.

7
00:00:29.282 --> 00:00:33.404
Shape DTOs for the boundary, not for internal convenience.

8
00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:34.825
Use System.

9
00:00:34.825 --> 00:00:36.247
Text.

10
00:00:36.247 --> 00:00:40.298
Json and other supported platform features intentionally.

11
00:00:40.298 --> 00:00:46.979
Document ownership, expected fields, versions, and consumers so future changes are reviewable.

12
00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:50.746
Treat deserialized data as untrusted until validated.

13
00:00:50.746 --> 00:01:00.340
Check required fields, business meaning, authorization context, object state, size, version, and compatibility before making decisions.

14
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:08.940
Keep parsing, validation, authorization, and business logic distinct enough that reviewers can see where trust is earned.

15
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.489
Avoid unsafe type handling and arbitrary runtime type selection.

16
00:01:13.489 --> 00:01:20.809
Where polymorphism is necessary, use explicit allow-lists of known contract types and clear validation.

17
00:01:20.809 --> 00:01:33.318
Review legacy serializers, broad compatibility shortcuts, custom converters, XML parser settings, caches, queues, plugin systems, imports, exports, and diagnostic object dumps.

18
00:01:33.318 --> 00:01:35.806
Finally, protect serialized output.

19
00:01:35.806 --> 00:01:43.197
Use minimization, projection, ignore rules, access control, retention, and secure transfer where needed.

20
00:01:43.197 --> 00:01:46.537
Secure serialization is not a one-time setting.

21
00:01:46.537 --> 00:01:52.792
It is an ongoing habit of making data boundaries deliberate, testable, and maintainable.
